staging: wfx: fix init/remove vs IRQ race
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Current code races in init/exit with interrupt handlers. This is noticed
by the warning below. Fix it by using devres for ordering allocations and
IRQ de/registration.

WARNING: CPU: 0 PID: 827 at drivers/staging/wfx/bus_spi.c:142 wfx_spi_irq_handler+0x5c/0x64 [wfx]
race condition in driver init/deinit

Cc: stable@vger.kernel.org
Fixes: 0096214a59a7 ("staging: wfx: add support for I/O access")
Signed-off-by: Michał Mirosław <mirq-linux@rere.qmqm.pl>
Reviewed-by: Jérôme Pouiller <jerome.pouiller@silabs.com>
Link: https://lore.kernel.org/r/f0c66cbb3110c2736cd4357c753fba8c14ee3aee.1581416843.git.mirq-linux@rere.qmqm.pl
Signed-off-by: Greg Kroah-Hartman <gregkh@linuxfoundation.org>

Here is the root cause:
The bug is caused by a race condition between the initialization and removal of the driver and the handling of interrupts. Specifically, if an interrupt occurs during the initialization or removal process, it can lead to undefined behavior since the driver may not be fully set up or may already be in the process of being torn down. This is particularly problematic in the context of the `wfx_spi_irq_handler`, which can be invoked while the driver is not in a stable state.

